dm-404 is made of parts under different licences, because it builds on
projects that are under different licences. Each part's licence text is in
LICENSES/.

  Part                                   Licence                 Why
  -------------------------------------  ----------------------  -------------------------------
  core/qemu/  (machine, devices,         GPL-2.0-or-later        it is part of QEMU (GPL-2.0);
   patches to QEMU)                                              each file carries an SPDX line
  core/fx/    (the effects engine)       MIT                     our own code; see below for the
                                                                 built library
  frontend/   (the app, the DM-404     AGPL-3.0-or-later       it is built on JUCE, which is
   Link plugin, tests)                                           AGPLv3 (or commercial)
  frontend/ThirdParty/fatfs/             FatFs licence (BSD-     ChaN's FatFs, unchanged but for
                                         style, in each file)    its configuration
  tools/, docs, everything else          MIT

What the built programs are under, when you pass them on:

  qemu-system-arm(.exe)   GPL-2.0-or-later: QEMU with core/qemu/. Offer the
                          corresponding source (QEMU v11.1.2 plus this repo).
  sp404fx.dll             MIT (core/fx) + MIT (DaisySP) + LGPL-2.1 (the parts
                          of DaisySP-LGPL it links statically: the compressor
                          and ReverbSc). The whole source is public, which
                          lets anyone rebuild or relink it, as the LGPL asks.
  DM-404(.exe),         AGPL-3.0-or-later, as JUCE's open-source licence
  DM-404 Link.vst3,     requires (the VST3 SDK in JUCE is MIT). Anyone who
  LinkHostTest            receives them may have the source (this repo).

Not part of dm-404, and never included in it:

  - Roland's SP-404MKII firmware (System Program), which the user downloads
    from Roland and the app copies in. It is Roland's copyright.
  - The Windows MIDI Services SDK runtime (Microsoft, MIT): the build uses
    its metadata to generate headers; at run time it comes from Windows, or
    the user supplies it.
  - Anyone's samples or projects.

"Roland" and "SP-404" are trademarks of Roland Corporation. dm-404 is an
independent project, not affiliated with or endorsed by Roland; the names
are used only to say what hardware it emulates.

SPDX: GPL-2.0-or-later AND LGPL-2.1-only AND AGPL-3.0-or-later AND MIT
